{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012FdgHunphCVvHMrjNSDkaH","type":"tool_result","content":"prompt.txt\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:13:  under .ard/ are left out. A last line says when the patch was cut at\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:64:  the directory named by $ARD_SIDECAR when it is set. The sidecar is\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:68:  git diff when git works here. diff.patch is the authoritative change.\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:92:   the worker's report, when they exist. When the user message lists\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:113:     worker's report explains is drift only when it is wrong;\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:128:   A missing test blocks only when it is high: risky or core logic, or a\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:131:   blocking when it is a real bug. low and info are never blocking.\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:132:6. The verdict is changes when at least one finding is blocking, and\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:148:  substitute a tool of your own choosing (for example go vet alone) when\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:150:  check. Only when the repository names none, fall back to the\n}\n\n// Valid reports whether s is one of the five severities.\nfunc (s Severity) Valid() bool { return s.Rank() > 0 }\n\n// Limits on scanner output. Any violation fails the whole run.\nconst (\n\tMaxLineBytes   = 1 << 20  // one JSONL line\n\tMaxStreamBytes = 16 << 20 // the whole stdout\n\tMaxFindings    = 10000\n\tMaxBodyBytes   = 64 << 10\n\tMaxFingerprint = 512 // bytes\n\tMaxTitleRunes  = 200\n\tMaxGroupBytes  = 256\n\tMaxFiles       = 1000\n\tmaxPathBytes   = 4096\n\tmaxErrorLines  = 20\n)\n\n// Finding is one line of scanner output.\ntype Finding struct {\n\tFingerprint string   `json:\"fingerprint\"` // required; stable across runs\n\tTitle       string   `json:\"title\"`       // required; one line\n\tSeverity    Severity `json:\"severity\"`    // required\n\tGroup       string   `json:\"group\"`       // optional; \"\" = one issue per finding\n\tFiles       []string `json:\"files\"`       // optional; repo-relative\n\tBody        string   `json:\"body\"`        // optional markdown\n}\n\n// DecodeError is one invalid line of scanner output.\ntype DecodeError struct {\n13:- **Scanners** — every X (default 4) hours; find problems, file or update issues, close what is fixed\n26:    S[\"Scanners<br/>every X h; dedupe by fingerprint, group up to N\"] -->|ard:pending| T[\"Triage<br/>lead routes to PM, architect, designer, docs, eng\"]\n166:## Scanners\n188:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\n451:| Scanners write only `.ard/<issue#>/` | The number does not exist until the issue is filed | File the issue first, then write its directory |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00922c60-179a-40b6-b4d5-d0c46e257df3","uuid":"17f873d2-586d-422a-ba51-761a833c46ad","timestamp":"2026-11-23T15:57:34.613Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"prompt.txt\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:13:  under .ard/ are left out. A last line says when the patch was cut at\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:64:  the directory named by $ARD_SIDECAR when it is set. The sidecar is\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:68:  git diff when git works here. diff.patch is the authoritative change.\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:92:   the worker's report, when they exist. When the user message lists\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:113:     worker's report explains is drift only when it is wrong;\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:128:   A missing test blocks only when it is high: risky or core logic, or a\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:131:   blocking when it is a real bug. low and info are never blocking.\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:132:6. The verdict is changes when at least one finding is blocking, and\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:148:  substitute a tool of your own choosing (for example go vet alone) when\n.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt:150:  check. Only when the repository names none, fall back to the\n}\n\n// Valid reports wh